  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Drought response Zambia in the project area of the Response Preparedness-III project area</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Currently Zambia is experiencing the driest agricultural season in more than forty years, resulting in substantial crop and animal losses, worsening the lives and livelihoods of communities in the country.  84 out of 116 districts of country have been affected by this crisis. On 29 February 2024, the President of Zambia declared a national emergency due to prolonged drought. On 16 April 2024, the joint rapid needs assessment and the Government Drought Response Appeal indicated that 6.6 million people needed urgent humanitarian assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency respond to drought affected families.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gwembe district, Southern Province</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This Drought Bilateral response aims at supporting 9,682 households (58,093people) affected by drought in Gwembe district of Southern Province by providing support around Food Security and livelihoods, Health and WASH. Additionally, the strategy will integrate community engagement and accountability/RCCE, as well as Protection, Gender and inclusion across all sectors, for the overall operational timeframe of 12 months</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This drought bilateral support targets 58,093 people and aims to reduce the negative impacts of drought and contribute to the improvement of food security, hygiene and health behaviors, and improve the nutrition status of children under the age of five. The response will target 58,093 people with WASH, health promotions and CEA/ RCCE/ PGI interventions whereas for cash and livelihoods, the target will be 30,000 people and 6000 people respectively. The operation will engage a total of 100 volunteers to champion the implementation. The Bilateral Support is part of a Federation-wide approach, based on the response priorities of the Operating National Society and in consultation with all Federation members contributing to the response.</narrative>
  </description>
